@@ -28,7 +28,7 @@ Extension discovery uses one Hayai-owned `ContentKind` policy at the J2K present
 
 APK repository URLs remain in J2K's single `extensionRepos` preference. Hayai stores only novel membership and novel-only ownership tags. Manga-only, novel-only, and shared ownership transitions are idempotent: removing one view never deletes a URL or its metadata while the other view still owns it. Repository redirects migrate both J2K URLs and Hayai tags together. The typed Manga/Novel repository controllers and Manga/Novel/Migration extension tabs are narrow UI adapters over those contracts; they do not introduce a second downloader or extension catalog.
 
-LNReader-compatible JavaScript sources use a separate Hayai-owned trust boundary. `AppModule` registers one manager. `SourceManager` merges the manager's source flow with J2K's authoritative APK source flow and rejects source-ID collisions. Repository documents and plugin downloads are bounded. Remote repositories require HTTPS, optional publisher checksums are enforced, and installed code and metadata are published atomically. Each source owns a serialized QuickJS runtime with evaluation, response-size, and cache limits. Installation initializes the runtime before code becomes active. It also caches filter and setting schemas so J2K's synchronous source UI never starts QuickJS on the main thread. Chapter documents expose an absolute base URL. Source-owned asset requests retain cookies and a referrer and enforce a 16 MiB stream limit. Users explicitly accept repository and install warnings because TLS and file integrity do not authenticate a repository publisher. The bridge exposes only the compatibility APIs needed by LNReader plugins, including network, Cheerio and Jsoup, storage, timers, crypto, URL, and protobuf helpers. It does not expose Android APIs or app lifecycle APIs.
+LNReader-compatible JavaScript sources use a separate Hayai-owned trust boundary. `AppModule` registers one manager. `SourceManager` merges the manager's source flow with J2K's authoritative APK source flow and rejects source-ID collisions. Repository documents and plugin downloads are bounded. `NovelPluginRepositoryDocument` normalizes current root arrays and legacy `plugins` or `sources` envelopes, removes invalid optional catalogue websites, and percent-encodes raw path brackets before URI validation. Remote repositories require HTTPS, optional publisher checksums are enforced, and installed code and metadata are published atomically. Each source owns a serialized QuickJS runtime with evaluation, response-size, and cache limits. Installation initializes the runtime before code becomes active. It also caches filter and setting schemas so J2K's synchronous source UI never starts QuickJS on the main thread. Chapter documents expose an absolute base URL. Source-owned asset requests retain cookies and a referrer and enforce a 16 MiB stream limit. Users explicitly accept repository and install warnings because TLS and file integrity do not authenticate a repository publisher. The bridge exposes only the compatibility APIs needed by LNReader plugins, including network, Cheerio and Jsoup, storage, timers, crypto, URL, and protobuf helpers. It does not expose Android APIs or app lifecycle APIs.
 
 ## Database reset and migration
 
@@ -46,7 +46,7 @@ The Advanced setting `eh_is_hentai_enabled` controls adult source discovery. Dis
 
 `SettingsSearchHelper` contains one registration seam for `NovelSettingsController`. The controller and all novel preferences remain under the Hayai namespace. This seam makes the full novel reader settings searchable without moving reader behavior into J2K. Novel language tools, custom-source building, APK extensions, JavaScript plugins, import/export, enhanced batch add, and E-Hentai account controls are J2K `SettingsController` screens. Their feature state, validation, jobs, and persistence remain Hayai-owned. Only reader and authenticated WebView lifecycles retain dedicated activities.
 
-`PreferencesHelper` has one compatibility seam that routes its enum-backed FlowPreferences through Hayai's non-throwing name decoder. Current enum names round-trip unchanged, known legacy aliases retain the closest supported behavior, and removed or future values use each preference's declared default without rewriting the stored value. This protects startup, shortcuts, widgets, settings, and presenters before they consume restored pre-reset preferences. The legacy `group_chapters_history_type=BySource` value currently projects to J2K's `BySeries`; exact source-section rendering remains an audited port rather than being represented by a nonfunctional enum entry. The complete reset-era preference comparison is recorded in `docs/development/legacy-preference-audit.md`.
+`PreferencesHelper` has one compatibility seam that routes its enum-backed FlowPreferences through Hayai's non-throwing name decoder. Current enum names round-trip unchanged, known legacy aliases retain the closest supported behavior, and removed or future values use each preference's declared default without rewriting the stored value. This protects startup, shortcuts, widgets, settings, and presenters before they consume restored pre-reset preferences. The legacy `group_chapters_history_type=BySource` value is a live enum member again and renders History source sections instead of projecting to `BySeries`. The complete reset-era preference comparison is recorded in `docs/development/legacy-preference-audit.md`.
 
 Browse and Recents share J2K's activity-owned tab strip. Their controller exit callbacks must not hide or clear that strip when the destination implements J2K's `TabbedInterface`; the entering controller remains responsible for replacing the tab contents. This keeps tab ownership inside existing J2K controller lifecycle and avoids changes to `MainActivity`.
 
@@ -93,6 +93,12 @@ sheet delegates source discovery and editing to Hayai, including installed sourc
 referenced by J2K manga rows, and unavailable sources that were hidden previously. This is strictly
 a presentation filter: J2K remains authoritative for every manga, chapter, history, and update row,
 and pagination advances over hidden rows so a page containing only hidden sources cannot loop.
+History `BySource` is an approved Recents presentation adapter. `RecentsPresenter` still loads J2K
+history rows, chapter expansion, download state, swipe actions, and reader routing. Hayai's
+`RecentSourceGrouper` owns only source-section ordering. The existing J2K
+`RecentMangaHeaderItem` renders source sections with `SourceManager.getOrStub`, `Source.icon()`,
+local source artwork, and a normal J2K popup action to add the section source to the same
+`RecentSourceVisibility` policy. No parallel source-header layout is introduced.
 
